✅ What is a Payload in Java (especially in APIs)?
A payload refers to the body/data sent in an HTTP request or response — usually in JSON format — when working with REST APIs.

For example:

json
Copy
Edit
{
  "username": "afham",
  "password": "secret123"
}
This is the payload sent to the server in a POST request.

✅ Validating Payload in Java (Spring Boot)
In Spring Boot, you typically use:

🔹 Step 1: Create a DTO (Data Transfer Object) with validation annotations
java
Copy
Edit
import jakarta.validation.constraints.*;

public class LoginRequest {

    @NotBlank(message = "Username is required")
    private String username;

    @NotBlank(message = "Password is required")
    private String password;

    // Getters & Setters
}
🔹 Step 2: Validate in Controller using @Valid
java
Copy
Edit
import org.springframework.validation.annotation.Validated;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api")
public class AuthController {

    @PostMapping("/login")
    public String login(@RequestBody @Valid LoginRequest request) {
        return "Login successful";
    }
}
🔹 Step 3: Handle Validation Errors
Spring will automatically return 400 Bad Request with error details if validation fails.
You can also customize this with @ControllerAdvice:

java
Copy
Edit
@RestControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(MethodArgumentNotValidException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<?> handleValidation(MethodArgumentNotValidException ex) {
        Map<String, String> errors = new HashMap<>();
        ex.getBindingResult().getFieldErrors().forEach(error ->
            errors.put(error.getField(), error.getDefaultMessage())
        );
        return new ResponseEntity<>(errors, HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST);
    }
}
🔸 Common Validation Annotations
| Annotation        | Purpose                           |
| ----------------- | --------------------------------- |
| `@NotNull`        | Field must not be `null`          |
| `@NotBlank`       | Field must not be `null` or empty |
| `@Size(min, max)` | Check string/collection size      |
| `@Email`          | Must be a valid email             |
| `@Min/@Max`       | Minimum/maximum number constraint |
| `@Pattern(regex)` | Must match the given regex        |


✅ Summary

Payload = data sent in request body.

Validate using @Valid and annotations from jakarta.validation.

Handle validation failures with @ControllerAdvice.